exists su name chirp draft prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing one chirp manuscript.

Goal:
- Compress the source into one useful idea people feel smarter sharing.
- Use the selected template family.
- Preserve the core arc: problem, hidden cause, insight.

Input notes:
- Candidate pools contain one candidate per line.
- Ignore blank lines.
- Use only surviving candidates from the pools.
- If REVISION_FEEDBACK is present, obey it.

Output rules:
- Output one chirp manuscript only.
- 100-280 characters total, including spaces and line breaks.
- Aim for 140-220 characters when possible.
- Prefer 2 short sentences over 3 unless a third line clearly helps.
- If a draft would run long, compress harder instead of adding another clause.
- 1 to 3 short sentences.
- Plain text only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.

Drafting rules:
- Keep it source-grounded.
- Slight surprise is good; unsupported novelty is not.
- You may use the proof hook only if it sharpens clarity without bloating the post.
- You may use the boundary only if it keeps the post honest without flattening it.
- Prefer concrete source wording over abstract ideological labels.
- Do not force a question.
- Do not assume what vague groups believe.
- Do not write a mini-thread.
- Do not add unsupported concrete facts, actors, dates, statistics, policies, or mechanisms..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name chirp source thrust verify prompt ob text quoted.text.Determine whether the TEXT stays grounded in the SOURCE.

Goal:
- Allow strong compression, rhetorical emphasis, and obvious implications.
- Fail only when the TEXT introduces unsupported concrete claims.

PASS if:
- The core idea is supported by the SOURCE.
- The TEXT is a faithful compression or rhetorical restatement.
- The TEXT adds framing or emphasis without inventing new concrete facts.

FAIL if:
- The TEXT adds a concrete fact, actor, date, statistic, policy, or mechanism not supported by the SOURCE.
- The TEXT materially changes what the SOURCE says.
- The TEXT turns a vague source into a stronger concrete claim without support.

Tie-break policy:
- If support is plausible and the wording is mainly rhetorical, choose PASS.
- If the TEXT states an obvious implication rather than a new concrete fact, choose PASS.
- Fail only on clear unsupported additions or clear distortion.

Output rules:
- First output one short paragraph (1-2 sentences, max 70 words) explaining the grounding judgment.
- Final line must be exactly PASS or FAIL.
- No bullets, no labels, no markdown.
- No text after the final verdict line..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name chirp source thrust verdict prompt ob text quoted.text.Read the verifier analysis and output exactly one word: PASS or FAIL.

Rules:
- Return FAIL only when the analysis clearly says the text adds unsupported concrete claims or materially distorts the source.
- Otherwise return PASS.
- One word only.
- No labels, no explanation, no punctuation..text.quoted be text ya

